## Why This Is Asked

Assess familiarity with Linux process scheduling, cgroups, systemd properties, and practical experimentation. The scenario emphasizes beginner-friendly diagnostic steps and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Linux CPU scheduling basics (CFS, CPU shares vs quotas)
- Systemd resource controls (CPUQuota, CPUQuotaPeriodUSec, cpuset)
- Tools: mpstat, pidstat, iostat, systemd-cgls

## Code Example

```javascript
#!/usr/bin/env node
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
const services = ['critical-api', 'worker-a'];
services.forEach(s=>{
  try{
    const quota = execSync(`systemctl show ${s} -p CPUQuota --value`, {encoding:'utf8'}).trim();
    const per = execSync(`systemctl show ${s} -p CPUQuotaPeriodUSec --value`, {encoding:'utf8'}).trim();
    console.log(`${s}: CPUQuota=${quota||'0'}, PeriodUSec=${per||'0'}`);
  }catch(e){
    console.error(`err ${s}:`, e.message);
  }
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Which steps would you automate to monitor for burst-induced latency and what signals would you alert on?
- What trade-offs exist between CPUQuota vs cpuset in terms of scheduling fairness and contention?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world WAL tail latency can throttle throughput in bursts; this question probes capability to diagnose IO path bottlenecks and apply production-grade mitigations that don’t compromise data integrity.

## Key Concepts
- IO scheduling and blk-mq
- Per-disk and per-tenant I/O control (cgroups v2)
- CPU/IRQ affinity and NUMA awareness
- WAL isolation strategies and PostgreSQL tuning
- Rigorous load-testing and safe rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js: simple iostat collector for WAL latency analysis
const {spawn} = require('child_process');
const log = require('fs').createWriteStream('/var/log/iostat_wal.log', {flags:'a'});
const proc = spawn('iostat', ['-x','1']);
proc.stdout.on('data', data => log.write(data));
proc.stderr.on('data', d => process.stderr.write(d));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify data durability isn’t compromised during rollback?
- Which metrics/logs would you baseline before and after changes, and how would you automate rollback if thresholds are not met?
